Okay, the latest test run still shows occasional flicker and corruption in the streaming region. The problem seems to be that the logic that trims the stream buffer (the “visible” portion) is still allowing too many rows to be kept, causing the rendered box to overflow its fixed height. When that happens the terminal UI redraws incorrectly – the bottom of the box gets overwritten and the cursor jumps.

What have I already changed?  
- I added `overflow="hidden"` to the outer `<Box>` so that any overflow is clipped rather than spilling out.  
- I split the transcript items into `staticItems` (the scrollback that lives in the docked region) and `liveItems` (the live stream that appears above the input).  
- I introduced `liveRef` and `staticRef` to keep mutable references to those arrays, and added a new `tailOnly` flag on `Item` to indicate when an oversized item has been truncated for display‑only purposes.  
- I also added a new `estimateRows` helper to compute how many terminal rows a given `Item` will occupy, taking wrapping into account.

Even with those improvements, the stream‑buffer trimming still uses a hard‑coded budget:
```ts
const budget = Math.max(4, rowsRef.current - 16);
```
The `-16` was a rough guess for the number of rows taken up by the UI chrome (header, input line, status bar, etc.). But the UI has changed: I now have separate static and live regions, and the header/footer heights are a bit different. Moreover, the budget should consider that each logical line may wrap to multiple rows, so simply subtracting a fixed number of rows is insufficient – especially when the terminal width is narrow.

A better heuristic is to compute the budget as roughly half of the available rows (leaving enough space for the static dock and the input area) and to round down to avoid overshooting. I can keep a minimum of 4 rows to ensure we always show something. So the new budget calculation becomes:
```ts
const budget = Math.max(4, Math.floor((rowsRef.current - 12) / 2));
```
Here, `rowsRef.current - 12` subtracts the estimated height of the static dock and input region (12 rows seems appropriate after the recent UI refactor). Dividing by 2 gives a conservative estimate of how many rows we can safely keep in the live stream without risking overflow, and `Math.floor` guarantees an integer.

All other parts of the trimming loop stay the same: we split `visible` into lines, walk backwards adding rows until we hit the budget, and then reassemble the kept lines into the new stream buffer.

